:"Egypt","kind":"Region (Country)","lat":26.4902,"lon":29.8808,"summary":"The land of the Nile and the pyramids, the oldest kingdom of which we have any record, holds a place of great significance in Scripture.","verses":564,"v":[2,3,6,9,10,12,14,19,20]},{"id":"nile_895","name":"Nile","kind":"Water (River)","lat":19.21141,"lon":30.56733,"summary":"Dark; blue, not found in Scripture, but frequently referred to in the Old Testament under the name of Sihor, i.e., “the black stream” (Isa. 23:3; Jer. 2:18) or simply “the river” (Gen. 41:1; Ex. 1:22, etc.) and the “flood of Egypt” (Amos 8:8).","verses":30,"v":[3,9]},{"id":"cush_312","name":"Cush","kind":"Region","lat":21.95979,"lon":31.34356,"summary":"A son, probably the eldest, of Ham, and the father of Nimrod (Gen. 10:8; 1 Chr. 1:10). From him the land of Cush seems to have derived its name.","verses":16,"v":[10]},{"id":"migdol_799","name":"Migdol","kind":"Settlement","lat":30.97269,"lon":32.44456,"summary":"A strongly-fortified place 12 miles from Pelusium, in the north of Egypt (Jer. 44:1; 46:14).","verses":6,"v":[10]},{"id":"syene_1120","name":"Syene","kind":"City","lat":24.06618,"lon":32.89586,"summary":"Opening (Ezek. 29:10; 30:6), a town of Egypt, on the borders of Ethiopia, now called Assouan, on the right bank of the Nile, notable for its quarries of beautiful red granite called “syenite.”","verses":3,"v":[10]},{"id":"pathros_925","name":"Pathros","kind":"Region","lat":28.32236,"lon":30.69231,"summary":"The name generally given to Upper Egypt (the Thebaid of the Greeks), as distinguished from Matsor, or Lower Egypt (Isa. 11:11; Jer. 44:1, 15; Ezek. 30:14), the two forming Mizraim.","verses":5,"v":[14]},{"id":"babylon_151","name":"Babylon","kind":"City","lat":32.53617,"lon":44.42082,"summary":"The Greek form of BABEL; Semitic form Babilu, meaning “The Gate of God.” In the Assyrian tablets it means “The city of the dispersion of the tribes.”","verses":255,"v":[18,19]},{"id":"tyre_1184","name":"Tyre","kind":"City"